irmed the energy intervention  making sure that households don't pay on  average more than two and a half  thousand pounds this winter and indeed  for the next two years for field you  remember just a few weeks ago people  were talking about energy bills this  winter of five six even seven thousand  Pounds a year and thanks to that  intervention last week by the Prime  Minister and by the chancellor that is  not going to happen so we've taken that  action to protect households at this  time of unprecedented some other  problems as I've said we've seen  um you know changes in or turbulence in  Global Financial markets for a number of  months not just here but around the  world the Bank of America corresponder  just a minute ago  um talked about the bank of England's  intervention yesterday of course the  bank of Japan intervened actually in  their currency Market uh just a few days  ago so other central banks are having to  take action as well the bank of England  did what it was supposed to acting of  course independently and it had its  desired effects and by the end of  yesterday  um the market had settled down again you  say it's a global problem the IMF hasn't  had to issue a stinging rebuke to any  other G7 country has it yeah look I saw  the imf's comments I respectfully uh  disagree with them the response that I'm  sort of most interested in is the  response by British business  organizations who represent the  businesses the British businesses that  are going to grow that are going to  create these jobs and if you look at  what organizations like the CBI the  confederation of British industry the  Federation of small businesses the  British Chambers of Commerce uh said  last Friday in response to the growth  plan all of them welcomed it really  strongly because speaking for their  members they can see it's going to grow  our economy okay so so business groups  liked it and would you agree that  although some of the tax cuts were well  received you just went 