e of  that measure  um it amounted to about to less than one  twentieth less than five percent of the  total fiscal measures it was a very  small part of the package most of the  measures like cutting National Insurance  and cutting the basic rate of income tax  which were the biggest the most  expensive measures they made up sort of  80 of it and those are targeted at  everybody everyone benefits you me  everybody but but the truth is the  markets weren't properly prepared were  they for what came at them and when you  when the mini budget was announced it  didn't have the obr's independent  assessment of what it meant for the  economy and how it was going to be paid  for and that spooked the markets they  were badly prepared weren't they and  that's why well I can't comment on  Marketplace headedness but I can no I  mean sorry the markets weren't prepared  for what you announced you didn't  prepare the markets well I think I mean  quite a few of the measures particularly  the more expensive ones like the energy  intervention to protect households the  National Insurance reversal and  corporation tax have been sort of quite  well trailed in advance  um but we are going to do of course a  full obr scoring in fact we're going to  do it twice this year as we're required  to do by law once on the 23rd of  November that's eight weeks away well  once on the 23rd of November wanted to  be done properly  um and and again with the spring budgets  there'll be two obr scorings this year  which is required by law  um we're also going to be setting out  the chancellor rather we'll set out his  medium-term fiscal plan which will  ensure that debt government debt as a  proportion of GDP the size of our  economy Falls over time uh because we  are going to do this responsibly and we  should remember of course that we start  this in a good position as a country  because our existing debt to GDP ratio  that's the measure most people use is  the second lowest in the G7 Jared Lyons  who you will know I'm sure often  referred to as Liz truss's favorite  Economist said that quasiquartang had  failed to adequately prepare the  financial markets ahead of this  announcement I'm looking said that  quasi-karting Chancellor is in uh  constant contact with the markets he's  meeting the governor of the bank of  England on a very regular basis he I  think he's yesterday he had meetings  together with his colleagues with  financial Market participants I think  he's had several meetings with them in  the last week or so um so I don't think  I accept that but he is in constant  contact with Market participants doesn't  the obr need to produce its report  sooner than the end of November well I  think we're going to do this just in an  orderly fashion we set a very clear date  the 23rd of November they'll do a full  scoring of uh of the measures that have  been taken they'll update their  forecasts let's just do it in a in a  measured organized way that is the right  way to approach this and that's what  we'll get on the the 23rd in addition to  our medium-term fiscal plan  has the government made any mistakes  uh well no one's perfect  um but I think I'm not going to sort of  apologize for having a plan to grow the  UK's economy which will increase  people's wages that will create new jobs  I'm certainly not going to apologize for  having an energy Intervention which is  protecting every single household in  this country from what could have been  devastating bills this winter you say no  one's perfect to acknowledge that some  mistakes were made well look I'm not  going to get into this sort of um well  why not post-facto uh raking over but  the point I want to make most clearly is  the one I just made which is having a  growth plan to grow jobs increase  people's wages make sure the UK is  internationally competitive introduce  supply side reforms things like the  transport infrastructure reducing  business business regulation all of that  will make the whole country ultimately  better off and I say the energy  intervention is protecting every single  household in this country from what  would otherwise have been devastating  bills you say it's going to make the  country better off but what about public  spending you've asked government  departments for efficiency savings  haven't you that's right so on the  public spending side side two points to  make on how this gets paid for I mean  clearly growth is part of it if we grow  the economy it generates more taxes how  long will that take and we set out  actually in the in the Blue Book last  week on page 27  um it illustrates that if we can get our  economy growing by one percent faster  than it otherwise would uh after five  years that generates 47 billion pounds  of additional um tax revenue but we're  also going to be responsible with public  spending as you just um sort of  suggested which means first of all uh  sticking within the existing spending  limits the so-called um spending Review  21 limits that was a review done about a  year ago it covered Three Financial  years we're in the first of those years  now we are going to stick to those  spending limits so the efficiency and  prioritization exercise that you  referred to  um is designed to uh do two things  firstly make absolutely sure we stick to  those spending limits and secondly make  sure that we're prioritizing expenditure  uh not on anything that's wasteful but  on things that really deliver Frontline  public services and drive economic  growth people have heard so many times  before efficiency savings being sought  from departments this basically means  that  all departments are going to face a  squeeze aren't they but we found about  three over three billion pounds of  efficiency savings last year as I say we  actually we're going to stick to the to  the the spending and so-called spending  envelope we're going to stick to those  planned spending levels that were set  out a year ago over the next three year  period but we're going to make sure that  we're spending the money in the best  possible way and we're going to make  sure we don't exceed those limits so  with inflation at 10 your money just  isn't going to go as far and and  departments like the NHS like the Health  Service just can't cope can they I mean  Charlie Bean former governor of the bank  of England said spending cuts made  necessary by this mini budget could  finish the NHS well the NHS has had a  record funding settlement and to be  clear the inflation  um that is felt by the public sector is  different to the inflation felt by sort  of you and I as just consumers I said  it's a lower figure and even despite the  higher inflation that we've seen  recently it's a global phenomenon caused  by Putin's illegal invasion of Ukraine  despite that the spending envelope the  spending levels that we're working  within will still see real terms  increases public spending adjusting for  public sector inflation but we are going  to stick absolutely  rigidly to those spending limits because  it is important to be financially  responsible a lot of people are fairly  horrified by what's been going on the  market it's not least Tory MPS who some  publicly a lot privately have talked  about their discomfort should  quasi-cotting resign no I don't think  you should the chancellor has I think  set out very clearly a vision for growth  to get our economy growing that is what  we need and if you think about just  think about this in the sort of medium  term what we need is an economy that is  delivering the jobs of the future  attracting people and companies from  around the world to uh who are  successful to to work and to and to  produce jobs here and that is the right  plan and he's and he's fixed and he and  the Prime Minister working together have  fixed this energy uh crisis for families  they would we would have had bills of  six thousand pounds that's you know they  intervened to stop that and that's the  right thing to do and I think families  up and down the country will be pleased  they've got a prime minister and a  chancellor who have taken  urgent decisive action to protect them  from the effects of Putin's War when it  comes to energy bills

